\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\PARstart{T}{he} Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) enables continuous
collection and sharing of medical data from wearable devices, clinical
sensors, imaging systems, ambulances, and hospital information systems.
Such data are increasingly outsourced to cloud--fog infrastructures to
support telemedicine, collaborative diagnosis, emergency care, and
large-scale healthcare analytics. However, outsourcing sensitive medical
records to semi-trusted infrastructures introduces significant challenges
in confidentiality, fine-grained cross-domain authorization, secure
retrieval, integrity verification, and regulatory compliance with HIPAA,
GDPR, and PDPA~\cite{ref1,ref2,ref3,ref4,ref5,ref20,ref21,ref47,ref50,ref51}.

Searchable encryption enables users to query encrypted outsourced data
without first decrypting the entire repository. Dynamic Searchable
Symmetric Encryption (DSSE) further supports record insertion, deletion,
and modification~\cite{ref29,ref30,ref31,ref37,ref38,ref42,ref45,ref46},
while verifiable searchable encryption employs authenticated structures
such as Merkle trees and blockchain commitments to detect forged or
modified search evidence returned by untrusted servers
~\cite{ref6,ref8,ref9,ref10,ref22,ref23,ref24,ref30,ref31,ref32,ref35,ref46}.
In parallel, multi-authority attribute-based mechanisms distribute
authorization across independent administrative domains
~\cite{ref26,ref27,ref28}, and recent LWE- and lattice-based approaches
investigate post-quantum protection for searchable data sharing
~\cite{ref16,ref41,ref52,ref54,ref55}. These advances address important
cryptographic requirements, but they do not fully resolve how
authorization evolution, dynamic encrypted indexes, distributed search,
and verifiable retrieval should interact in a large multi-domain IoMT
deployment.

A fundamental challenge is that \emph{authorization and searchable-index
states evolve together but at different scopes}. Consider encrypted IoMT
records distributed across hospital, laboratory, and emergency-care
domains. A cardiologist may query \emph{``ECG records associated with
arrhythmia''}, while an emergency physician may search \emph{``cardiac
records requiring emergency intervention''} across multiple domains.
Such queries should access only shards whose policies are satisfied and
whose relevant authority states are current. However, state-independent
tokens may permit stale authorization, whereas binding indexes to a
global authorization version causes an unrelated AA update to invalidate
unaffected indexes. This motivates policy-state-aware tokens and
commitments bound only to the AAs governing each policy.

A second challenge is efficient distributed encrypted search. A
cross-domain query may span shards maintained by different Fog Search
Nodes (FSNs), while the least-loaded FSN may lack a required shard,
maintain stale authority state, or incur high index and proof-generation
costs. Resource-only load balancing therefore poorly reflects encrypted
search overhead. Scheduling should instead consider \emph{index locality,
policy-state synchronization, verification workload, and queue delay}
when assigning authorized shards.

IoMT data and authorization are also inherently dynamic: records are
inserted, modified, or deleted, while sharing policies and user attributes
may change or be revoked. These events have different scopes. A record
modification changes its encrypted content and searchable entries, whereas
an attribute revocation may leave ciphertexts unchanged but invalidate
indexes governed by the affected AA. For example, revocation by a research
AA should not update emergency records governed only by hospital and
emergency AAs. Global re-keying, index reconstruction, or FSN-wide
synchronization therefore introduces unnecessary overhead.

Finally, dynamic evolution complicates verifiable retrieval. A record
modification produces a new content-addressed encrypted object, whereas a
policy or revocation change may require only updated policy-state tokens,
index roots, and commitments. A returned $CID_i$ alone cannot prove that
its index entry belongs to the current authenticated index or policy
state. Hence, the framework must distinguish \emph{data evolution} from
\emph{authorization evolution}, update only affected dependencies, and
cryptographically bind searchable state to the exact encrypted object and
current policy state.
To address these challenges, we propose
\textbf{MA-LB-PQ-VDSE}
(\emph{Multi-Authority Load-Balanced Post-Quantum Verifiable Dynamic
Searchable Encryption}), a cloud--fog framework for scalable multi-domain
IoMT retrieval. MA-LB-PQ-VDSE decouples content-addressed encrypted
records from a policy-state-aware dynamic index. A Version-Bound
Authorization Profile (VAP) captures user-specific multi-authority state,
while policy-state digests bind search tokens only to the AAs governing
each policy, preventing unrelated AA updates from invalidating unaffected
indexes. The Adaptive Authorization-Aware Search Scheduler (AASS) assigns
authorized shards according to cryptographic, synchronization, and queue
costs, while Dependency-Aware Incremental Authorization Synchronization
(DIAS) updates only affected policies, records, shards, users, and FSNs.
Merkle proofs and blockchain commitments bind returned index entries to
their encrypted objects and current policy states, while ML-KEM provides
post-quantum protection for attribute-key delivery.

The main contributions are summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}

\item \textbf{Policy-state-aware multi-authority dynamic search.}
We develop VAP and a policy-state-aware dynamic index that bind search
tokens and authenticated commitments only to the current states of
policy-governing AAs. This supports authorized cross-domain search while
preventing unrelated authority updates from invalidating unaffected index
entries.

\item \textbf{Adaptive authorization-aware distributed scheduling.}
We design AASS to assign each required index shard to an eligible Fog
Search Node according to predicted index traversal, proof generation,
policy-state synchronization, and queue costs, thereby incorporating
cryptographic and authorization overhead beyond resource-only load
balancing.

\item \textbf{Dependency-aware dynamic evolution and synchronization.}
We introduce DIAS to support data and authorization changes according to
their dependency scope. It incrementally updates only affected policies,
records, index shards, users, and FSNs, avoiding global index
reconstruction and authorization-state redistribution.

\item \textbf{Decoupled verifiable outsourcing and protected key delivery.}
We decouple encrypted IoMT objects from evolvable search indexes using
content-addressed references, allowing policy changes without
re-outsourcing unchanged ciphertexts. ML-KEM protects attribute-key
delivery, while authenticated encryption, Merkle proofs, and
blockchain-anchored commitments provide integrity- and
authorization-consistent retrieval.

\end{itemize}


