\subsection{Blockchain-Assisted Secure Healthcare and IoMT Data Sharing}

Blockchain has been widely adopted to provide decentralized trust, immutable auditing, and fine-grained access control for healthcare and IoMT data sharing. Malamas \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref1}, Gao \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref2}, Chen \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref4}, and Yuan \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref5} proposed blockchain-based architectures for secure medical data sharing and decentralized access management. Fugkeaw~\cite{ref3} introduced an efficient policy update mechanism for outsourced health records, while Fugkeaw \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref21} incorporated adaptive revocation and fog-assisted load sharing for IoT healthcare systems. More recently, Gao \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref47}, Hak and Fugkeaw~\cite{ref50}, and Deebak and Hwang~\cite{ref51} extended blockchain-assisted healthcare sharing toward cloud-edge IoMT environments. Although these approaches improve decentralized trust and access control, they primarily focus on secure storage and authorization, offering limited support for efficient dynamic searchable encryption and scalable encrypted retrieval across multiple administrative domains.

\subsection{Dynamic and Verifiable Searchable Encryption}

Searchable Symmetric Encryption (SSE) originated with Song \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref39} and was formally strengthened by Curtmola \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref40}, while Thingom~\cite{ref41} introduced forward-secure searchable encryption. Recent Dynamic Searchable Symmetric Encryption (DSSE) schemes have significantly improved dynamic updates, forward/backward privacy, expressive queries, and multi-user support~\cite{ref29,ref33,ref35,ref36,ref37,ref38,ref42,ref45}. To ensure search correctness, numerous verifiable searchable encryption (VSE) schemes have integrated Merkle authentication, authenticated data structures, blockchain auditing, and cryptographic proofs~\cite{ref7,ref8,ref9,ref10,ref11,ref12,ref13,ref14,ref22,ref23,ref24,ref25,ref30,ref31,ref32,ref43,ref46}. These studies substantially improve security and verifiability; however, search execution generally remains centralized, verification is largely decoupled from authorization management, and the schemes do not consider workload-aware encrypted search in large-scale IoMT environments.

\subsection{Multi-Authority Authorization and Searchable Encryption}

Fine-grained authorization across multiple organizations has been extensively studied using Attribute-Based Encryption (ABE). Bethencourt \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref27} first introduced CP-ABE, which has become the foundation of secure healthcare data sharing. Xu \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref26} proposed authorized encrypted search for multi-authority medical databases, while Yu \textit{et al.}~\cite{ref28} combined blockchain with revocable multi-authority ABE for Industrial IoT. Other studies explored certificateless searchable encryption~\cite{ref19}, LWE-based multi-writer searchable encryption~\cite{ref16}, multi-user searchable encryption~\cite{ref17}, divertible searchable encryption~\cite{ref18}, and multi-client encrypted search~\cite{ref15}. Despite these advances, existing multi-authority schemes primarily concentrate on cryptographic authorization and key management, while overlooking dynamic searchable encryption over continuously generated IoMT data and intelligent workload balancing across distributed fog-cloud infrastructures.

Overall, existing research has advanced blockchain-assisted healthcare sharing, dynamic and verifiable searchable encryption, and multi-authority authorization independently. However, no existing framework jointly integrates multi-authority authorization, dynamic searchable encryption, verifiable encrypted retrieval, blockchain-backed auditing, and adaptive load-balanced search execution for large-scale IoMT data sharing. These limitations motivate the proposed framework.

Table~\ref{tab:comparison} compares representative searchable encryption frameworks with the proposed scheme. Existing studies have addressed dynamic searchable encryption, verifiable retrieval, multi-authority authorization, blockchain-assisted auditing, or lattice-/post-quantum cryptography individually, but none integrates these capabilities within a unified framework. Xu \textit{et al}.~\cite{ref26} and Yu \textit{et al}.~\cite{ref28} support multi-authority access control but lack verifiable encrypted search and workload-aware search execution. Representative DSSE and VDSE schemes~\cite{ref29,ref35,ref46} improve dynamic updates, search privacy, and retrieval verification, yet generally assume centralized search processing and do not provide blockchain-backed auditing or quantum-resistant security. Recent lattice-/post-quantum approaches~\cite{ref16,ref41,ref52,ref54} strengthen cryptographic resilience against quantum adversaries but primarily focus on secure searchable-encryption constructions rather than scalable authorization management, verifiable retrieval, and distributed search orchestration. Blockchain-assisted healthcare frameworks~\cite{ref47,ref51} enhance decentralized trust and auditability but do not jointly support dynamic searchable encryption and verifiable retrieval, while the load-balanced framework of Perera and Fugkeaw~\cite{ref48} improves search scalability without supporting multi-authority authorization, verifiable retrieval, or quantum-resistant cryptography. In contrast, the proposed framework is the first to unify dynamic searchable encryption, verifiable retrieval, multi-authority authorization, blockchain-backed auditing, lattice-/post-quantum secure communication, expressive Boolean search, and adaptive authorization-aware load-balanced search execution within a single architecture for large-scale cross-domain IoMT data sharing.
\section{Our Proposed System}

This section presents the architecture of
\textbf{MA-LB-PQ-VDSE}, a multi-authority,
load-balanced, post-quantum verifiable dynamic searchable
encryption framework for large-scale IoMT data sharing.
The framework enables fine-grained authorization,
dynamic encrypted search, efficient index maintenance,
verifiable retrieval, and adaptive workload distribution
across distributed fog-cloud infrastructures.

\subsection{System Model}

Fig.~\ref{fig:system} illustrates the MA-LB-PQ-VDSE architecture for
secure and verifiable IoMT data sharing across multiple healthcare
domains. The framework integrates multi-authority authorization,
policy-state-aware encrypted search, adaptive fog scheduling, dynamic
synchronization, and blockchain-assisted verification.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{MA-LB-PQ-VDSE System Model.png}
    \caption{Overall architecture of the proposed MA-LB-PQ-VDSE framework.}
    \label{fig:system}
\end{figure*}

\begin{itemize}

\item \textbf{IoMT Devices and Gateways:}
IoMT devices generate healthcare data, while edge gateways aggregate and
preprocess the collected records before secure outsourcing.

\item \textbf{Data Owner (DO):}
A healthcare provider acting as the DO encrypts IoMT records, defines
multi-authority access policies, outsources encrypted objects to IPFS,
and constructs policy-state-aware searchable indexes and commitments.

\item \textbf{Data User (DU):}
Authorized healthcare personnel generate search requests, verify returned
search evidence, retrieve encrypted records, and decrypt data when their
multi-authority attributes satisfy the corresponding access policies.

\item \textbf{Attribute Authorities (AAs):}
Independent AAs manage domain-specific attributes, user registration,
attribute-key issuance, revocation, and authorization-state commitments.
Their independent states collectively support cross-domain authorization
without a centralized authority.

\item \textbf{Authorization Index Manager (AIM):}
The AIM maintains user authorization metadata, policy mappings, VAPs, and
current multi-authority states. It validates search authorization, derives
policy-state-aware query tokens and authorized shard sets, and coordinates
dependency-aware state updates through DIAS.

\item \textbf{Load Balancing Controller (LBC):}
The LBC executes AASS to assign required search shards to eligible FSNs
according to index locality, policy-state synchronization, predicted
verification workload, and queue delay.

\item \textbf{Fog Search Nodes (FSNs):}
Distributed FSNs maintain searchable-index shards, execute encrypted
search over assigned authorized shards, synchronize affected states, and
generate Merkle authentication evidence for returned index entries.

\item \textbf{InterPlanetary File System (IPFS):}
IPFS stores encrypted IoMT objects off-chain using content-addressed
identifiers ($CID$s), decoupling large ciphertext storage from the
evolvable searchable index and blockchain metadata.

\item \textbf{Consortium Blockchain:}
The blockchain anchors authority states, policy-state-aware index
commitments, Merkle roots, and dynamic-update metadata. It provides an
authenticated reference for authorization-state freshness and
search-result verification without storing IoMT ciphertexts on-chain.

\end{itemize}

