For the proposed framework, token generation scales with
$|\mathcal{T}_Q|$, since Phase~VI generates policy-state-aware tokens
for the queried keywords under the authorized policies. Search then
performs direct token lookups only over the shards selected by AASS,
giving $O(|\mathcal{T}_Q|)T_L$ rather than scanning the global index.

Dynamic-update cost is localized to the $k$ affected entries. Updating
their policy-state-bound tokens and record-level Merkle authentication
state requires at most $O(k\log t)T_H$, where $t$ is the number of
indexed keywords for the affected record. Likewise, verification of
$r$ returned entries requires $O(r\log t)T_H$ for their Merkle paths
and $O(r)T_{\rm BC}$ blockchain-state reads.

DIAS synchronization depends only on the changed policy-relevant
authority components and affected index entries, yielding
$O(a+k\log t)T_H$. Unrelated policies, records, and FSNs incur no update
cost. Thus, the proposed framework shifts online processing toward
policy-scoped hash and index operations while avoiding global index
reconstruction and system-wide authorization synchronization.
\subsection{Performance Evaluation}

\subsubsection{Experimental Setup}

The proposed framework was implemented in Python~3.11 and evaluated
on Amazon AWS EC2 c6i.xlarge instances, each equipped with 4~vCPUs
and 8~GB RAM. Four Fog Search Nodes (FSNs) and one cloud server were
deployed within an AWS Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) to emulate a
distributed cross-domain IoMT healthcare environment. Each FSN
maintains an authorization-aware searchable index and performs
encrypted search for its administrative domain, while the cloud
server stores encrypted EHRs and associated blockchain metadata.

The implementation employs ML-KEM-768 for post-quantum key
establishment, AES-256-GCM for symmetric encryption, SHA-256 for
hashing and Merkle-tree construction, and Hyperledger Fabric~v2.5
for maintaining authorization commitments and audit records.
Search indexes combine bitmap-based authorization-aware filtering
with authenticated Merkle trees to support efficient encrypted
retrieval and result verification.

Experiments were conducted using the Synthea synthetic healthcare
dataset. Encrypted searchable indexes containing $10^4$--$10^6$ EHRs were generated from the dataset and uniformly
distributed across four administrative healthcare domains. Unless
otherwise specified, each query contains five keywords. Each
experiment was repeated 10 times, and the reported results represent
the mean with 95\% confidence intervals.

The proposed framework was compared with four representative
searchable encryption schemes: Scheme~\cite{ref30} (Peony++),
Scheme~\cite{ref35}, Scheme~\cite{ref41}, and
Scheme~\cite{ref54} (LV-PQ-ABSE). The evaluation examines trapdoor
generation, encrypted-search efficiency, cross-domain search
scalability, keyword-update performance, result verification,
authorization synchronization, and authorization-aware load
balancing under increasing workloads.


\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 1: Policy-State-Aware Token Generation}}
\label{exp:trapdoor}

This experiment evaluates the online cost of generating policy-state-aware
search tokens. The number of queried keywords is varied as
$q\in\{1,5,10,15,20\}$, while the number of authorized policies is varied
as $|\mathcal{P}_U|\in\{1,2,4,8\}$. The proposed framework is compared
with Guo \textit{et al}.~\cite{ref35}, XB-Muse~\cite{ref36}, Thingom
\textit{et al}.~\cite{ref41}, and Zhuang \textit{et al}.~\cite{ref52}.
ML-KEM key establishment is excluded because it is performed during
attribute-key delivery rather than for each search request.

For every authorized policy $P_\ell\in\mathcal{P}_U$, the AIM derives
the current policy-state digest
\begin{equation}
PV_\ell=H(\mathsf{Encode}(\mathbf{V}_{P_\ell}))
\end{equation}
and generates, for each queried keyword $w_r$,
\begin{equation}
T_{r,\ell}^{Q}
=
H(w_r\parallel PID_\ell\parallel PV_\ell\parallel Dom_\ell)
\end{equation}
Hence, the number of generated tokens is
\begin{equation}
|\mathcal{T}_Q|
=
q|\mathcal{P}_U|
\end{equation}
when all queried keywords are evaluated under all authorized policies,
and the dominant token-generation cost is
\[
O(|\mathcal{T}_Q|)T_H
\]

\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{images/fig_exp1_trapdoor.pdf}
    \caption{Policy-state-aware token-generation latency under increasing
    query and authorization scopes.}
    \label{fig:exp1}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:exp1} shows that the proposed scheme exhibits approximately
linear growth with the number of generated policy-aware tokens. Increasing
$q$ enlarges the keyword dimension of the query, while increasing
$|\mathcal{P}_U|$ expands the number of policy states under which those
keywords must be encoded. Nevertheless, token generation remains
hash-dominated and avoids pairing, exponentiation, or lattice operations
in the online search path.

Compared with schemes whose trapdoor construction involves
attribute-dependent group operations or more expensive cryptographic
processing, MA-LB-PQ-VDSE maintains relatively low online preparation
cost. More importantly, the additional computation directly reflects
the authorization scope: only policies satisfying
$\mathcal{S}_U\models P_\ell$ contribute tokens. Thus, token-generation
overhead scales with the effective authorized search scope rather than
the total number of policies or domains in the system.


\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 2: Search Latency}}
\label{exp:search}

This experiment evaluates encrypted-search scalability as the searchable
index size increases from $10^4$ to $10^6$ encrypted records. The
proposed framework is compared with Scheme~\cite{ref30},
Scheme~\cite{ref35}, Scheme~\cite{ref41}, and
Scheme~\cite{ref54}. The query size, number of participating domains,
and authorization scope are fixed, while query selectivity is kept
constant. Hence, the number of matching records increases proportionally
with the index size, allowing the experiment to capture both encrypted
index lookup and output-sensitive result processing under increasing
data volume. For Scheme~\cite{ref30}, this setting is consistent with
its output-sensitive search complexity $O(n_w^l)$.

\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{images/fig_exp2_search.pdf}
    \caption{Encrypted-search latency versus searchable-index size.}
    \label{fig:exp2}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:exp2} shows that search latency increases with index size
for all evaluated schemes, while MA-LB-PQ-VDSE exhibits slower latency
growth. Before search execution, the Authorization Index Manager
validates the current VAP and derives the policy-state-aware token set
$\mathcal{T}_Q$ together with the required authorized shard set
$\mathcal{S}_Q$. AASS then assigns each required shard only to an
eligible FSN maintaining that shard. Consequently, online search is
restricted to policy-authorized shards rather than traversing the
global searchable repository.

For a fixed query and authorization scope, the dominant index-access
cost is approximately
\[
O(|\mathcal{T}_Q|)T_L
\]
while the measured latency additionally grows with the number of matching
entries returned under the constant-selectivity workload. Thus, increasing
the global index size does not proportionally increase the number of
index partitions that must be searched; instead, the main growth arises
from output-sensitive result processing within the selected shards.

Scheme~\cite{ref30} scales with the number of accessible matching
records, Scheme~\cite{ref54} combines encrypted-index lookup with
candidate filtering, while Schemes~\cite{ref35} and~\cite{ref41}
incur their respective query-dependent cryptographic processing.
Overall, the results show that policy-state-aware token lookup and
authorized shard selection limit unnecessary index processing and
improve encrypted-search scalability over large EHR collections.


\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 3: Cross-Domain Search Scalability}}
\label{exp:crossdomain}

This experiment evaluates encrypted-search latency as the number of
participating healthcare domains $d$ increases from 2 to 10. The
proposed framework is compared with Schemes~\cite{ref30},
\cite{ref35}, \cite{ref41}, and \cite{ref54}. Since these baselines do
not provide equivalent authorization-aware cross-domain orchestration,
their domain-local searches are independently executed and the results
aggregated. The query size and per-domain index size are fixed to isolate
cross-domain search overhead.

\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{images/fig_exp3_crossdomain.pdf}
    \caption{Cross-domain search latency versus number of participating domains.}
    \label{fig:exp3}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:exp3} shows increasing latency as more domains participate,
with MA-LB-PQ-VDSE exhibiting slower growth. The AIM first validates the
current VAP and derives policy-state-aware tokens and the required
authorized shard set $\mathcal{S}_Q$. AASS then assigns each required
shard to an eligible FSN maintaining that shard, so search execution is
limited to authorization-valid index partitions rather than all
participating domain indexes.

As $d$ increases, the baselines accumulate domain-local search overhead
because their search procedures are independently executed across the
participating domains. In contrast, MA-LB-PQ-VDSE restricts execution to
the shards required by the authorized query and exploits distributed
shard processing through AASS. The results therefore demonstrate better
cross-domain scalability by reducing unnecessary index processing as the
number of participating healthcare domains increases.
\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 4: Fine-Grained Verification Effectiveness}}
\label{exp:verify}

This experiment evaluates the overhead and granularity of verifiable
retrieval as the number of returned entries $r$ increases from 10 to
1000. Controlled tampered or stale entries are also injected to evaluate
whether invalid results can be individually identified without rejecting
otherwise valid results. We report the average verification latency
$T_{\rm avg}=T_{\rm verify}/r$, invalid-result localization rate, and
valid-result retention. The proposed framework is compared with
Schemes~\cite{ref30}, \cite{ref35}, and \cite{ref54}, which provide
explicit retrieval-verification mechanisms.

\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{images/fig_exp4_verify.pdf}
    \caption{Fine-grained verification effectiveness:
    (a) average verification latency per returned entry and
    (b) invalid-result localization and valid-result retention.}
    \label{fig:exp4}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:exp4}(a) shows that MA-LB-PQ-VDSE incurs a moderate
per-result verification cost. For each returned entry $I_{i,j}$, the DU
reconstructs
\begin{equation}
L_{i,j}=H(\mathsf{Encode}(I_{i,j}))
\end{equation}
and verifies its Merkle path against $Root_i$. It then checks the latest
blockchain-anchored metadata and recomputes
\begin{equation}
Commit_i^*
=
H(CID_i\parallel Root_i\parallel PID_i
\parallel PV_i\parallel AuthState_i)
\end{equation}
The resulting verification cost is dominated by
$O(r\log t)T_H+O(r)T_{\rm BC}$, where $t$ denotes the number of indexed
keywords in the corresponding record-level Merkle tree.

Fig.~\ref{fig:exp4}(b) demonstrates the benefit of per-entry verification.
Because each returned entry is accompanied by its own authentication
evidence, a modified, stale, or policy-state-inconsistent entry can be
localized and rejected while valid entries remain acceptable. In
contrast, schemes relying primarily on aggregated verification evidence
provide less direct fault localization at individual-result granularity.

Overall, MA-LB-PQ-VDSE introduces additional per-result verification
overhead in exchange for fine-grained integrity and freshness checking.
This enables corrupted or stale results to be isolated without
discarding the entire response, which is particularly useful for
cross-domain IoMT retrieval.



\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 5: Dynamic Index Update}}
\label{exp:update}

This experiment evaluates dynamic index-update latency as the number of
modified keyword--record pairs $k$ increases from $10^2$ to $10^5$.
MA-LB-PQ-VDSE is compared with Scheme~\cite{ref30} (Peony++) and
Scheme~\cite{ref35}, which explicitly support dynamic searchable-index
updates. The total index size is fixed to isolate the effect of increasing
update volume.

\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{images/fig_exp5_update.pdf}
    \caption{Dynamic index-update latency versus number of modified
    keyword--record pairs.}
    \label{fig:exp5}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:exp5} shows increasing update latency for all schemes,
while MA-LB-PQ-VDSE maintains lower growth as the update batch increases.
Scheme~\cite{ref30} follows its $O(k)$ update behavior, whereas
Scheme~\cite{ref35} additionally incurs per-keyword hash/PRF processing
and puncturable-PRF operations.

For MA-LB-PQ-VDSE, an update modifies only the affected searchable-index
entries and their Merkle authentication paths. For each changed entry,
the policy-state-aware token and leaf
$L_{i,j}=H(\mathsf{Encode}(I_{i,j}))$ are recomputed, followed by
incremental Merkle maintenance. The dominant cost is therefore
approximately
\[
O(k\log t)T_H
\]
where $t$ denotes the number of indexed keywords in the corresponding
record-level Merkle tree. Unchanged entries and unrelated records require
no reconstruction.

The results demonstrate that localized index evolution and incremental
authenticated-state maintenance enable MA-LB-PQ-VDSE to efficiently
support large dynamic update batches without rebuilding the complete
searchable index.



\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 6: DIAS Synchronization Ablation}}
\label{exp:sync}

This experiment evaluates the effectiveness of Dependency-Aware
Incremental Authorization Synchronization (DIAS) as the fraction of
policies affected by an authority-state update increases from 10\% to
100\%. Three configurations are compared: (i) \emph{Full-State
Synchronization}, which reconstructs and propagates the relevant
authorization/index state to all FSNs; (ii) \emph{Incremental-All},
which updates only affected state but propagates the resulting deltas
to all FSNs; and (iii) \emph{DIAS}, which updates only dependent
policies, records, index entries, and Merkle paths and propagates the
deltas only to FSNs maintaining affected shards. Synchronization
latency and transferred synchronization data are measured.

\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]
    {images/fig_exp6_sync.pdf}
    \caption{DIAS synchronization under increasing affected-policy ratio.}
    \label{fig:exp6}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:exp6} shows that DIAS achieves the lowest synchronization
overhead, particularly when authority updates affect only a small
fraction of policies. An update by $AA_k$ is restricted through the
dependency chain
\begin{equation}
AA_k
\rightarrow\mathcal{P}_k^{\rm aff}
\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_k^{\rm aff}
\rightarrow\mathcal{S}_k^{\rm aff}
\rightarrow\mathcal{F}_k^{\rm aff}
\end{equation}
so unrelated policies, records, shards, and FSNs require no update.
Incremental-All reduces state-reconstruction overhead but still incurs
unnecessary propagation, whereas Full-State Synchronization processes
substantially more state.

As the affected-policy ratio approaches 100\%, the advantage of DIAS
narrows because a larger portion of the system becomes dependency
relevant. The results therefore confirm that DIAS gains its efficiency
from both dependency-localized state maintenance and selective
propagation, with the largest benefit under sparse authorization
changes typical of multi-authority environments.



\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 7: AASS Search Throughput}}
\label{exp:throughput}

This experiment evaluates AASS under increasing concurrent search
workloads. Four scheduling strategies are compared: (i) \emph{No Load
Balancing}; (ii) \emph{Round Robin}; (iii) \emph{Least Loaded}, which
considers current workload but not shard locality or synchronization
state; and (iv) \emph{AASS}. Concurrent queries increase from 100 to
5000, while the number of FSNs and per-query workload are fixed.

For each query, AASS assigns every required shard
$S\in\mathcal{S}_Q$ only to eligible FSNs maintaining that shard. Among
replicated eligible nodes, it selects the FSN minimizing
\begin{equation}
SC_j=
\lambda_1C_j^{index}
+\lambda_2C_j^{verify}
+\lambda_3C_j^{sync}
+\lambda_4C_j^{queue}
\end{equation}

\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{images/fig_exp7_throughput.pdf}
    \caption{Search throughput under increasing concurrent workloads.}
    \label{fig:exp7}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:exp7} shows that AASS sustains substantially higher
throughput than unbalanced scheduling as concurrency increases, reaching
982 queries/s against 543 at 10{,}000 concurrent queries, while Least
Loaded attains the highest raw throughput of the four (1{,}030
queries/s). Without load balancing, requests concentrate on individual
FSNs and cause early saturation. Round Robin improves workload
distribution but ignores shard locality and current node state, while
Least Loaded reduces queue congestion without considering whether the
selected FSN maintains the required shard or requires synchronization.

AASS instead restricts each shard assignment to eligible FSNs and selects
the node with the lowest predicted search cost. By jointly considering
index-processing, verification, synchronization, and queueing overhead,
AASS reduces unnecessary forwarding and avoids overloaded or
synchronization-expensive nodes. The resulting trade is explicit: AASS
accepts a small throughput margin relative to Least Loaded in exchange
for eliminating cross-node forwarding entirely
(Fig.~\ref{fig:exp8}(c)), which Least Loaded cannot do because it
disregards shard locality. Combining locality with workload- and
state-aware scheduling therefore buys correctness of placement at a
bounded throughput cost, rather than dominating on throughput alone.

\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experiment 8: Load-Balancing Effectiveness}}
\label{exp:balance}

This experiment further evaluates AASS by examining workload distribution
across FSNs as concurrent queries increase from 100 to 10,000. The four
strategies in Experiment~\ref{exp:throughput} are compared using:
(i) standard deviation of FSN utilization; (ii) maximum FSN utilization;
and (iii) cross-node forwards. A cross-node forward occurs when a
scheduler assigns a required shard to an FSN that does not maintain it,
requiring redirection to an eligible node.

\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{images/fig_exp8_balance.pdf}
    \caption{Load-balancing effectiveness under increasing concurrent
    workloads: (a) utilization standard deviation, (b) maximum FSN
    utilization, and (c) cross-node forwards.}
    \label{fig:exp8}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:exp8}(a) shows that AASS maintains substantially lower
utilization variation than unbalanced scheduling (0.090 against 0.392 at
10{,}000 concurrent queries) and improves as load grows, indicating more
balanced workload distribution across eligible FSNs. Least Loaded attains
the lowest variation of the four (0.019), since it optimises queue
occupancy directly and is unconstrained by shard locality; AASS is
constrained to eligible FSNs and is therefore balancing over a smaller
candidate set at each assignment. Round Robin and Least Loaded improve
upon no load balancing but do not jointly account for required-shard
locality, synchronization state, and predicted processing cost.
Consequently, Fig.~\ref{fig:exp8}(b) shows that AASS also limits
processing hot spots as concurrency increases.

The locality benefit is further demonstrated in
Fig.~\ref{fig:exp8}(c). Conventional strategies may assign work to an
FSN that does not maintain the required shard, causing cross-node
forwarding. AASS instead considers only FSNs satisfying
$S\in\mathcal{S}_j$ for each required shard $S\in\mathcal{S}_Q$ and
selects the eligible node with the lowest predicted cost. It therefore
eliminates unnecessary forwarding -- measuring zero cross-node forwards
at every concurrency level, against approximately 2{,}100 for each of the
three conventional strategies at 10{,}000 concurrent queries -- while
maintaining balanced utilization. Together with
Experiment~\ref{exp:throughput}, these results show that AASS improves
distributed-search scalability through shard-local, state-aware, and
workload-aware scheduling, and that its distinguishing property is the
elimination of misplaced shard assignments rather than a uniform
advantage on throughput or utilization spread.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper presented \textbf{MA-LB-PQ-VDSE}, a post-quantum
authorization-aware framework for secure and scalable cross-domain
encrypted EHR retrieval in IoMT environments. The framework integrates
Version-Bound Authorization Profiles (VAPs), Policy-Driven Searchable
Indexes (PDSIs), authorization-aware search resolution, Adaptive
Authorization-Aware Search Scheduling (AASS), Incremental Authorization
Synchronization (IAS), and blockchain-anchored verification into a
unified retrieval architecture. By resolving authorization before
encrypted matching, MA-LB-PQ-VDSE restricts search execution to
permitted domains and effective candidate sets, while AASS exploits
authorization validity, index locality, synchronization state, and
workload information to improve distributed search execution. IAS
further limits authorization-update overhead through incremental state
maintenance and selective propagation to affected Fog Search Nodes. The security and performance analyses demonstrate that these mechanisms
provide scalable dynamic search while preserving authorization
consistency and verifiability. In particular, the proposed verification
mechanism associates independent authenticated evidence with each
returned ciphertext, enabling a tampered, stale, or authorization-inconsistent result to be individually identified and
rejected rather than invalidating the entire result set. Experimental
results further demonstrate scalable trapdoor generation, encrypted
search, cross-domain retrieval, authenticated dynamic updates, and
authorization synchronization. The AASS experiments also show improved
search throughput and workload distribution under increasing
concurrency, while fine-grained per-result verification provides
stronger validation at a moderate additional verification cost. Future work will investigate
larger-scale heterogeneous IoMT deployments, adaptive optimization of
authorization and scheduling policies, and further reduction of
verification and synchronization overhead for highly dynamic
cross-domain environments.


